WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Cut myself shaving for the 761st time.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.420
Stupid shaving, always ruining everything.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.520
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.340
So 2,000 years ago, something like half of people died before the age of five.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:16.460
1,800 years later, in 1800, the global under-five mortality rate had gone from somewhere around 50%

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.740
all the way down to somewhere around 43%.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.880
But then, it began declining dramatically.  Like, by 2013, the global child mortality rate wasn't

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.280
50% or 43%, it was 3.4%.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.380
In wealthy countries, it's under 1%.  How did this happen? Well, in the new annual letter

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.120
from Melinda and Bill Gates, Bill argues persuasively that the key to these big changes

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.680
in health and development was, and is, energy.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.280
The whole letter is fascinating (you should read it, there's a link in the dooblydoo)

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.800
but I want to highlight a few big points.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.260
So okay, there are about 1.3 billion people who don't have regular access to the kinds of energy

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.220
that most of us take for granted, and that has a huge impact on their lives.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.860
Like, without electricity, kids can't study after dark, except by candlelight,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.520
and they can't access online resources, like Wikipedia.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.200
Without tractors and energy-intensive fertilizer, people grow less food.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.240
And without running water, it's almost impossible to sterilize health care centers

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.220
so it's no surprise that the 18% of humans living without regular electricity

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.220
are more likely to die of preventable diseases and malnutrition

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.620
and much less likely to have access to good schools.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:21.080
They are also, by the way, disproportionately affected by climate change, which is especially unfair.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.860
You know, because by and large they haven't experienced that much benefit

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.300
from the fossil fuel consumption that's a big driver of climate change.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.220
For instance, the average person in Bangladesh

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.240
uses 98.8% less oil than the average American.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.880
And therein lies what I think will be the biggest question of this century:

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.440
how do we get energy solutions to the 1.3 billion people who don't yet have them

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.460
while also dramatically reducing carbon emissions?

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.360
The answer? Lots and lots of people trying lots and lots of solutions, most of which will fail,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.320
but a few of which will hopefully succeed spectacularly.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.500
Otherwise, we're in big trouble.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.080
So Hank, back in 1800 you probably would have been one of the 43% of people who didn't live to age 5;

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.820
you had a bunch of serious infections as a kid.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.880
This would have been bad news for you, obviously,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.280
but it also would have been somewhat bad news for the rest of us,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.420
because you've gone on to do some cool stuff.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.320
Companies you've started employ a lot of people,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.880
you've helped me and many others understand important scientific concepts,

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.740
you invented 2D-GLASSES which allow people to see 3D movies in a crisp two dimensions, et cetera.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.460
And basically, advances in health and development have allowed the world to contain more Hanks,

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.040
which is great, but now we need even more of them.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.280
We need as many Hanks as we can get!

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.100
Because we know from history that when lots and lots of innovators work on big problems,

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.560
they find big solutions.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.080
And that leads me to the second half of this year's Gates letter, which is about time.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.620
Specifically, how poverty is worsened by gender disparities when it comes to time.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.560
So Melinda Gates points out that globally,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.880
women spend twice as much time on unpaid work as men.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.900
This gap is largest in poor countries, where work like getting water, chopping wood, cooking, and cleaning

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.080
is mostly done by women.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.360
And as Melinda points out, assigning most unpaid work to women harms everyone,

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.300
because of what's known as opportunity cost.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:03.900
When girls are walking hours every week to get water, those are hours they can't spend in school or studying.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.760
If men and women are equally able to innovate and create, and they are,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.960
gender bias definitionally limits economic growth.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.760
Gender bias isn't just wrong ethically, it means less innovation,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.280
fewer jobs, and less inclusive governance.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.320
To address this problem, Melinda advocates recognizing, reducing, and redistributing.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.000
First, we need to recognize that unpaid work is work,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.320
second, we need to reduce the time needed to do such work,

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.260
and third, we need to redistribute that work more evenly between women and men.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.880
Hank, I think we're gonna look back on energy and gender equity

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.780
as two of the biggest challenges of the 21st century.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.240
But right now we're not just struggling to solve those problems;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.180
too often we're refusing to acknowledge they exist.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.420
And that has to change now, if the next 200 years are going to continue the successes of the previous 200.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.920
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

